 | D135 | **Microsoft-format gaps that openpyxl/python-docx/python-pptx cannot write (xlsx pivot tables, docx tracked changes, pptx transitions) are closed by editing the library's own already-written zip/element tree with `lxml`, never by forking the library.** Validated first as three standalone spikes (structurally consistent, read correctly by LibreOffice, and surviving a load-edit-save round trip through the library's own code), then built as three new workspace packages — `claudia-computes-io`, `claudia-composes-io`, `claudia-creates-io` — each depending on `claudia-core`, its one app package and its one format library (`openpyxl`, `python-docx`, `python-pptx`, as `>=` floors, matching the repo's existing pin style, not exact pins). Each package pairs a minimal base export from the app's own model (`workbook_to_xlsx`, `text_to_docx`, `slides_to_pptx`) with the one additive extension the spike covers (`add_pivot_table`, tracked changes folded into `text_to_docx` from `TextModel`'s own open suggestions, `add_transition`); `text_to_docx` is the one place a suggestion's `original`/`replacement` becomes real Word markup instead of being silently dropped. None of the three is in `scripts/api_surface.py`'s frozen `PACKAGES` list yet (same "first slice" status as `claudia_computes`/`claudia_composes`). | 2026-11-06 | Resolves the brainstorm on import/export architecture: researched library gaps (not "openpyxl has no cell styling," which was a wrong premise, but real gaps in pivot-table write support, track changes and transitions), ruled out forking or a shared `python-xlsx` (no thin OOXML layer exists to extract; xlsx's own gaps are business-logic, not packaging), chose "extend additively" over the alternative of putting the format libraries inside `claudia-core` (refused by the kernel's own purity tests — `claudia-core` does no I/O and knows no format) via `AskUserQuestion`. `odfdo` is deliberately **not** a dependency of any of the three packages yet: D70 (see also D5, D67, D69) wants ods/odt/odp import and export before xlsx/docx/pptx in every app, and nothing here is that story — adding the dependency without a spiked ODF gap to extend would be dead weight. The full ODF-first, loss-reporting import/export of E6.9/E9.5/E10.3 remains open. |
 
+## Restore with consent, and the line picker (twenty-third round)
+
+Building E3.7/E3.8 together: a live session's consent flow for restoring a checkpoint or switching to a hidden
+line, and a first version of the picker that surfaces those lines. Two decisions, agreed with the owner before
+building. **Note on numbering:** `D135` was independently allocated three times in this round's merge (Row height
+and column width; The Manage menu; Additive xlsx/docx/pptx extensions) — a real DOC-01 collision across concurrently
+merged branches, pre-existing in this merge and not introduced or fixed here. This round picks the next number clear
+of all of them (`D141`) rather than add a fourth collision; the three-way D135 collision itself is left for a
+dedicated renumbering pass.
+
+| ID | Decision | Date | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| D141 | **A new kernel primitive, `Document.peek_line(line)`: a read-only look at any line's state at its head, on a scratch model, without switching to it.** Owner, asked directly ("is a `peek_line()` in the core the way to do this?"): "Yes, let's have a `peek_line()` in the core." | 2026-11-07 | A thin wrapper around the existing `materialize`; touches nothing else (no mutation of the active line, undo steps or checkpoints). Lets the Lines picker (D52) show a hidden line's content before anyone proposes switching to it, instead of requiring a switch first and a switch back if it turns out to be the wrong one. |
+| D142 | **The consent itself — who must agree, the open proposal, the policy for silence at the deadline — is session state, not document state, and lives in `claudia-server` (a new `RestoreCoordinator`), not the kernel.** Every wire method it needs sits on the shared `core_methods` table, the same table `history`/`chat`/`lines` already sit on, so composes and creates get restore-with-consent for free the same way they already get history and chat. | 2026-11-07 | Consistent with D51/D52: the kernel already restores and switches outright (`restore_checkpoint`, `switch_to`); nothing about *whether it is allowed to happen right now* belongs in a document that might be opened by a batch job or a single person with nobody to ask. `RestoreCoordinator` is plain, dependency-free Python (27 tests in isolation, no kernel, no sockets) owned by `HostedDocument`, exactly as ephemeral as presence and locks: an open proposal and the one-time "what happened while you were away" notice live only as long as the document is held open in memory, with nothing to recover after a restart. Reached from the stateless wire methods through `Context`, the same injection pattern already used for `users` and `directory`. The frontend mirrors the placement: the new session methods and the consent banner live in `base-session.ts`/`SidePanel.vue`, the layer every app already shares for history and chat, not duplicated per app. |
